  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 213 PM MDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nThe long term period begins with approximately southwesterly flow \noverhead at 500-mb Saturday morning. A shortwave trough embedded in \nthis flow looks to support a surface low pressure traversing the \nforecast region during the afternoon and evening hours. \nThunderstorms are possible along a cold front behind the low. NBM 24 \nhr precipitation guidance suggests a 60-80% chance for greater than \n0.1 inches of precipitation Saturday across nearly the entire area. \nCurrently, thunderstorms are not expected to become severe, as GFS \nand EC model soundings show less than 30 kts of sfc-500mb vertical \nwind shear across the forecast area. LREF guidance is in support of \nthis solution, suggesting a 1 in 3 chance or less that vertical wind \nshear exceeds 30 kts. If severe thunderstorms were able to occur, \nthey would be most likely across Red Willow County in Nebraska and \nNorton County in Kansas, particularly if a 500-mb jet streak to the \nnorth can move into this region. However, confidence remains low \n(around 5%) that severe thunderstorms will occur Saturday. Highs are \ncurrently forecast in the 80s and lower 90s, save for eastern parts \nof Graham and Gove Counties in Kansas where highs may reach into the \nmid 90s. \n\nGEFS and EC ensembles are in support of a ridge building in \nthroughout the day Saturday from the South-Central Plains all the \nway to the Pacific Northwest. This ridge looks to be fully developed \nby Sunday morning, and is expected to be in place through at least \nMonday night. The ridge axis would initially be just west of the \nforecast area, with a surface high downstream in the Dakotas and \nMidwest. Northerly/northeasterly winds on Sunday associated with \nthis surface high may allow high temperatures to drop into the upper \n70s to mid 80s across the forecast area Sunday and Monday. \nThunderstorms may continue during this period as well. A surface low \nacross the Four-Corners Region would support southerly flow across \nthe Southern Plains, meeting the northerly flow in Kansas to create \na convergence zone. NBM 24 hr precipitation guidance continues to \nshow a 60-80% chance for greater than 0.1 inches of rain Sunday, but \ndrops to a 30-50% chance on Monday when the surface high to the \nnorth moves into the Midwest and allows southerly flow to dominate \nin Kansas. Even so, LREF guidance continues to show a 25% chance or \nless that vertical wind shear will exceed 30 kts across the forecast \nregion, limiting the severe risk. Confidence in severe weather \noccurring either Sunday or Monday is less than 5%. \n\nForecast uncertainty begins to increase by Tuesday. Ensemble \nguidance is in support of the ridge fully moving overhead and \nremaining in place for the remainder of the long term period, with \ntroughing remaining offshore to the west. However, GFS and EC \ndeterministics suggest that the trough may move in faster, moving \ninto the Rockies/Northern High Plains Region by Tuesday night. This \nis represented in the 500-mb height spread guidance which shows a \nlarge amount of spread developing over this region going into \nTuesday. If this trough can move in, a surface low could develop in \nthe North-Central Plains, establishing southwesterly flow across the \nforecast area. It would then move southeastward with time, crossing \nthe forecast area Wednesday. If the ridge is able to remain in place \nduring this period, highs in the upper 80s to lower 90s could be \nexpected Tuesday and Wednesday. However, if the trough can move \ninland Tuesday and develop a surface low, conditions on Tuesday \ncould be hotter, with drier conditions as well. A cold front would \nthen be possible on Wednesday as the low crosses the area. LREF \nguidance is about 75-80% in favor of the ridge remaining in place \nduring this period, which would suggest conditions simply remaining \nwarm.",
